# verify-__APP__

This skill runs the built app, feature by feature, and keeps what the app
printed. The unit tests show that the parts work; this shows that the app does.

## Run it

Run each command from the project root, as its own command, with no pipe.

```bash
python3 .claude/skills/verify-__APP__/verify doctor
python3 .claude/skills/verify-__APP__/verify run
```

`run` builds the app, runs the steps of each feature in a fresh temp folder,
and removes that folder at the end. `run --only <id>` runs one feature.

## Read the result

Read the exit code and the last line together. One without the other is not a
result.

| Exit | Last line starts with | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | `verify-__APP__: pass` | Every step held. |
| 1 | `verify-__APP__: summary` | A step failed or could not start. The lines above say which. |
| 2 | `verify-__APP__: error` | The run could not finish. It says nothing about the app. |

Anything else is not a result either: another exit code, another last line, a
run that was cut off. Report that as unverified, never as a pass.

A pass covers a commit only when all of these hold:

- the commit on the pass line is the output of `git rev-parse HEAD`;
- the pass line does not end in `dirty` (uncommitted changes) or `partial`
  (`--only` left features out).

Run it again after each new commit. An earlier result does not cover it.

## Evidence

The line `evidence .verify/runs/<run>` names the run folder. It holds
`run.json` and the output of the build and of every step, and it stays after
the run. Name that folder in your report, and quote from its files, not from
memory. `.verify/` is ignored by git.

## After a run that was cut off

```bash
python3 .claude/skills/verify-__APP__/verify runs
python3 .claude/skills/verify-__APP__/verify clean
```

`runs` lists each run with its state: `done`, `live`, `cut-off` (its runner
ended before the run finished) or `not-cleaned` (the run finished, printed a
line `not-removed`, and its temp folder is still there).

`clean` stops what a cut-off run left behind and removes those temp folders.
It acts only when the runner of a run has ended for certain; any other run
counts as live and is left alone. It keeps every run folder.

## The feature map

`features.json`, beside this file, lists the features and the steps that prove
each one. When you add or change a feature of the app, change its entry in the
same pull request.

- A step is one command and what must be true after it: the exit code, and
  text that the output must hold or must not hold.
- Expect something that is false when the feature is broken. An exit code
  alone seldom is.
- The app must not touch real data. Point it at `{tmp}` through `env`, and put
  the files it needs in a `fixture` folder beside the map.
- `{root}` is the project and `{bin}` is the folder of the built program.
  `{tmp}` is a fresh folder for each feature, with its own copy of the
  fixture, so a feature gives the same result alone and in a full run.
- The runner refuses a key that it does not know, so a typing error cannot
  turn a check off.
- The map is code: the runner runs every command that it names, with your
  environment. Review a change to it as you review a change to the tests.
